          </svg>
        </div>
        <div class="legend"><span>Singapore</span><span>Foreign markets*</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="panel spikes">
        <h2>Three biggest spikes</h2>
        <div class="spike"><div class="spike-date">20-26 Jul</div><p><strong>Trigger:</strong> USTR imposed a 12.5% Section 301 tariff linked to forced-labour import bans. <strong>First foreign pickup:</strong> AP on 23 Jul (Wiseman/Anderson), then AP Asia reaction and Bloomberg/Yahoo; domestic outlets followed within hours.</p></div>
        <div class="spike"><div class="spike-date">17-23 Aug</div><p><strong>Trigger:</strong> the 4th India-Singapore Ministerial Roundtable and new food, telecoms and maritime cooperation. <strong>First foreign pickup:</strong> ANI/PTI-linked Indian feeds from 18 Aug, then Business Standard in Hindi and English.</p></div>
        <div class="spike"><div class="spike-date">24-30 Aug</div><p><strong>Trigger:</strong> Gan's Washington visit plus PM Wong's warning that trade is now security-driven. <strong>First foreign pickup:</strong> no material original foreign pickup in the top Meltwater results; the cycle remained Singapore-led.</p></div>
      </section>

      <section class="panel watch">
        <h2>Foreign reporter watch</h2>
        <p><strong>Paul Wiseman / Mae Anderson, AP:</strong> U.S. tariff law, deadlines and domestic cost; Singapore is one affected economy.</p>
        <p><strong>Elaine Kurtenbach / Chan Ho-him, AP Asia:</strong> regional reaction, legitimacy of forced-labour claims and legal durability.</p>
        <p><strong>Yee Ong, Bloomberg:</strong> Singapore's pushback and immediate market/economic exposure.</p>
        <p><strong>David Hutt, DW:</strong> EU-ASEAN trade architecture, supply-chain diversification and strategic de-risking.</p>
        <p><strong>Fathur Rochman / Aditya Ramadhan, ANTARA:</strong> tangible bilateral agreements, trust and Indonesian delivery priorities.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="actions">
        <h2>What differs, what is emerging, what to do</h2>
        <p><span class="num">1</span><strong>Largest divergence:</strong> domestic media foregrounds calibration, firm/worker support and policy trade-offs; overseas media foregrounds Singapore's utility as capital, logistics, technology and an FTA precedent.</p>
        <p><span class="num">2</span><strong>Under-covered locally:</strong> the EU “bilateral-deals-first” pathway to an ASEAN-wide framework is gaining foreign traction, while Singapore coverage remains meeting- and statement-led.</p>
        <p><span class="num">3</span><strong>Press implication:</strong> pair every rules-based-trade message with one concrete overseas benefit; pre-brief AP/Reuters/Bloomberg and specialist regional writers before travel; publish same-day Mandarin/Bahasa/Japanese fact sheets with tariff exposure, exemptions and Singapore's red lines.</p>
      </section>
    </aside>
  </main>

